<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8"/>
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/>
<title>CF Training | Module 01: The T.I.</title>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Barlow+Condensed:wght@400;600;700;800;900&family=Barlow:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;1,400;1,600&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/>
<style>
:root {
  --bg: #141210;
  --surface: #1c1917;
  --card: #242017;
  --border: #2e2a24;
  --border-light: #3a3530;
  --copper: #B87C4C;
  --copper-light: #C9A07A;
  --copper-dim: rgba(184,124,76,0.12);
  --copper-glow: rgba(184,124,76,0.06);
  --tan: #BF9B6F;
  --white: #F5F0E8;
  --grey: #7a7570;
  --dim: #9a9490;
  --red: #c0392b;
  --green: #6aaa64;
  --grad: linear-gradient(135deg, #B87C4C 0%, #C9A07A 50%, #BF9B6F 100%);
  --grad-h: linear-gradient(90deg, #B87C4C, #C9A07A);
}
*{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0;padding:0;}
html{scroll-behavior:smooth;}
body{background:var(--bg);color:var(--white);font-family:'Barlow',sans-serif;line-height:1.65;overflow-x:hidden;}

/* ── PROGRESS BAR ── */
.top-bar{position:fixed;top:0;left:0;right:0;height:3px;background:var(--border);z-index:9999;}
.top-bar-fill{height:100%;background:var(--grad);width:0%;transition:width .4s ease;}

/* ── HERO ── */
.hero{min-height:100vh;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;align-items:flex-start;padding:80px 60px;position:relative;overflow:hidden;}
.hero::before{content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;background:radial-gradient(ellipse 80% 60% at 70% 50%, rgba(184,124,76,0.07) 0%, transparent 70%);}
.hero-eyebrow{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:800;color:var(--copper);letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:20px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;}
.hero-eyebrow::before{content:'';display:block;width:40px;height:2px;background:var(--copper);}
.hero-num{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:clamp(100px,18vw,200px);font-weight:900;line-height:.85;color:transparent;-webkit-text-stroke:1px rgba(184,124,76,0.15);position:absolute;right:-20px;top:50%;transform:translateY(-50%);pointer-events:none;user-select:none;}
.hero-title{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:clamp(48px,7vw,90px);font-weight:900;line-height:.95;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:-1px;margin-bottom:24px;}
.hero-title em{font-style:normal;background:var(--grad);-webkit-background-clip:text;-webkit-text-fill-color:transparent;}
.hero-desc{max-width:560px;font-size:17px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:40px;}
.hero-stats{display:flex;gap:40px;margin-bottom:50px;}
.stat{display:flex;flex-direction:column;}
.stat-num{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:36px;font-weight:900;color:var(--copper);line-height:1;}
.stat-label{font-size:11px;color:var(--grey);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:2px;margin-top:4px;}
.hero-btn{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;background:var(--grad);border:none;padding:16px 32px;color:white;font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;border-radius:4px;cursor:pointer;transition:.2s;}
.hero-btn:hover{transform:translateY(-2px);box-shadow:0 8px 30px rgba(184,124,76,0.3);}
.scroll-hint{position:absolute;bottom:40px;left:60px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;color:var(--grey);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;}
.scroll-line{width:40px;height:1px;background:var(--border-light);}

/* ── LAYOUT ── */
.module-wrap{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 40px 120px;}

/* ── SECTION HEADERS ── */
.section-header{padding:80px 0 40px;}
.s-eyebrow{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:800;color:var(--copper);letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;margin-bottom:16px;}
.s-eyebrow::after{content:'';flex:1;max-width:60px;height:1px;background:var(--copper);opacity:.4;}
.s-title{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:clamp(32px,5vw,48px);font-weight:900;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:-0.5px;line-height:1.05;margin-bottom:12px;}
.s-sub{font-size:16px;color:var(--dim);max-width:680px;line-height:1.6;}

/* ── LESSON CARDS ── */
.lesson-grid{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;}
.lesson-card{background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:0;overflow:hidden;transition:.2s;}
.lesson-card:first-child{border-radius:10px 10px 0 0;}
.lesson-card:last-child{border-radius:0 0 10px 10px;}
.lesson-header{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:20px;padding:22px 28px;cursor:pointer;user-select:none;}
.lesson-header:hover{background:var(--copper-glow);}
.lesson-num{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:800;color:var(--copper);letter-spacing:2px;min-width:36px;}
.lesson-title-text{font-size:16px;font-weight:700;flex:1;}
.lesson-tag{font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:var(--copper);background:var(--copper-dim);padding:4px 10px;border-radius:20px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;}
.lesson-arrow{color:var(--grey);font-size:12px;transition:.3s;font-style:normal;}
.lesson-card.open .lesson-arrow{transform:rotate(180deg);color:var(--copper);}
.lesson-card.open .lesson-header{border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);}
.lesson-body{display:none;padding:30px 28px 28px;}
.lesson-card.open .lesson-body{display:block;}
.lesson-body p{color:var(--dim);font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;margin-bottom:16px;}
.lesson-body p:last-child{margin-bottom:0;}
.lesson-body strong{color:var(--white);font-weight:700;}
.lesson-body em{color:var(--tan);font-style:italic;}

/* ── CALLOUTS ── */
.callout{border-radius:8px;padding:20px 24px;margin:20px 0;font-size:15px;}
.callout-rule{border-left:3px solid var(--copper);background:var(--copper-dim);color:var(--copper-light);}
.callout-script{border-left:3px solid var(--tan);background:rgba(191,155,111,0.08);color:var(--tan);font-style:italic;line-height:1.7;}
.callout-warn{border-left:3px solid var(--red);background:rgba(192,57,43,0.08);color:#e07060;}
.callout-tip{border-left:3px solid #4a9a6a;background:rgba(74,154,106,0.08);color:#7ac09a;}
.callout-label{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:10px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;opacity:.7;}

/* ── DIALOGUE ── */
.dialogue-block{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;padding:24px;margin:20px 0;overflow:hidden;}
.dialogue-title{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:800;color:var(--copper);letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:20px;padding-bottom:12px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);}
.line{display:flex;gap:14px;margin-bottom:16px;align-items:flex-start;}
.line:last-child{margin-bottom:0;}
.speaker{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;min-width:100px;padding-top:2px;}
.speaker.prospect{color:var(--grey);}
.speaker.consultant{color:var(--copper);}
.speaker.front-desk{color:var(--tan);}
.line-text{font-size:15px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.65;}
.line-text em{color:var(--white);font-style:normal;font-weight:600;}

/* ── MISTAKES SECTION ── */
.mistakes-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:12px;margin-top:8px;}
.mistake-card{background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:8px;padding:20px;}
.mistake-label{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:10px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:10px;}
.mistake-label.bad{color:var(--red);}
.mistake-label.good{color:var(--green);}
.mistake-text{font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--dim);}
.mistake-text strong{color:var(--white);}

/* ── ROLEPLAY ── */
.roleplay-card{background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;margin-bottom:16px;}
.roleplay-header{padding:20px 24px;background:var(--copper-dim);display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;}
.roleplay-scenario{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--copper);}
.roleplay-type{font-size:11px;color:var(--grey);}
.roleplay-body{padding:24px;}
.roleplay-prospect{font-size:15px;font-style:italic;color:var(--tan);margin-bottom:16px;padding:14px 18px;background:rgba(191,155,111,0.06);border-radius:6px;}
.roleplay-objective{font-size:14px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.6;}
.roleplay-objective strong{color:var(--white);}
.coaching-note{margin-top:14px;padding:14px 18px;background:rgba(184,124,76,0.08);border-radius:6px;font-size:13px;color:var(--copper-light);line-height:1.6;}
.coaching-note::before{content:'COACHING NOTE  ';font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;font-size:10px;display:block;margin-bottom:4px;opacity:.7;}

/* ── DIVIDER ── */
.divider{height:1px;background:var(--border);margin:60px 0;}
.divider-label{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;color:var(--grey);font-size:11px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:60px 0;}
.divider-label::before,.divider-label::after{content:'';flex:1;height:1px;background:var(--border);}

/* ── STUDY COMPLETE ── */
.study-complete-banner{background:var(--copper-dim);border:1px solid rgba(184,124,76,0.3);border-radius:12px;padding:30px;text-align:center;margin:40px 0;display:none;}
.study-complete-banner h3{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:900;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--copper);margin-bottom:8px;}
.study-complete-banner p{color:var(--dim);font-size:14px;margin-bottom:20px;}

/* ── QUIZ ── */
.quiz-section{background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:16px;overflow:hidden;margin-top:40px;}
.quiz-locked{padding:60px 40px;text-align:center;}
.quiz-locked-icon{font-size:40px;margin-bottom:16px;opacity:.3;}
.quiz-locked h3{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:900;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--grey);margin-bottom:8px;}
.quiz-locked p{color:var(--grey);font-size:14px;}
.quiz-active{padding:40px;}
.quiz-active.hidden{display:none;}
.q-progress-wrap{height:3px;background:var(--border);border-radius:2px;margin-bottom:30px;overflow:hidden;}
.q-progress-bar{height:100%;background:var(--grad);width:0%;transition:.4s;}
.q-meta{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;margin-bottom:24px;}
.q-counter{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:800;color:var(--copper);letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;}
.q-topic{font-size:11px;color:var(--grey);}
.q-text{font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:var(--white);margin-bottom:28px;line-height:1.4;}
.q-options{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;}
.q-opt{background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:8px;padding:16px 20px;color:var(--dim);font-size:15px;text-align:left;cursor:pointer;transition:.2s;font-family:'Barlow',sans-serif;}
.q-opt:hover{border-color:var(--copper);color:var(--white);background:var(--copper-glow);}
.name-panel{padding:40px;}
.name-panel h3{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:900;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);margin-bottom:6px;}
.name-panel p{color:var(--dim);font-size:14px;margin-bottom:24px;}
.cf-input{background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--border);color:var(--white);padding:14px 18px;width:100%;border-radius:8px;font-family:'Barlow',sans-serif;font-size:16px;margin-bottom:16px;outline:none;transition:.2s;}
.cf-input:focus{border-color:var(--copper);}
.cf-btn{background:var(--grad);border:none;padding:16px 32px;color:white;font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:15px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;border-radius:8px;cursor:pointer;width:100%;transition:.2s;}
.cf-btn:hover{opacity:.9;transform:translateY(-1px);}
.results-panel{padding:40px;display:none;}
.result-score{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:72px;font-weight:900;line-height:1;margin-bottom:8px;}
.result-pass{color:var(--copper);}
.result-fail{color:var(--red);}
.result-label{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--grey);margin-bottom:24px;}
.missed-section{margin-top:24px;}
.missed-label{font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:800;color:var(--dim);letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:12px;}
.missed-item{background:rgba(192,57,43,0.07);border-left:3px solid var(--red);border-radius:0 6px 6px 0;padding:14px 16px;margin-bottom:8px;}
.missed-q{font-size:14px;color:var(--dim);margin-bottom:6px;}
.missed-a{font-size:14px;color:var(--tan);font-weight:600;}

/* ── FOOTER ── */
.module-footer{text-align:center;padding:60px 40px;border-top:1px solid var(--border);}
.module-footer p{color:var(--grey);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;}

/* ── RESPONSIVE ── */
@media(max-width:700px){
  .hero{padding:80px 24px 60px;}
  .module-wrap{padding:0 20px 80px;}
  .mistakes-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;}
  .hero-stats{gap:24px;}
  .hero-num{display:none;}
}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="top-bar"><div class="top-bar-fill" id="topFill"></div></div>

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-num">01</div>
  <div class="hero-eyebrow">City Fitness Training  ·  Deep Module Series</div>
  <h1 class="hero-title">The Art of<br>the <em>T.I.</em></h1>
  <p class="hero-desc">The Telephone Inquiry is your first shot. Before they ever walk through the door, you are already either building trust or losing them. This module covers everything — from your opening line to the moment they commit to showing up.</p>
  <div class="hero-stats">
    <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">8</span><span class="stat-label">Core Lessons</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">4</span><span class="stat-label">Full Scripts</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">4</span><span class="stat-label">Role-Play Scenarios</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">16</span><span class="stat-label">Quiz Questions</span></div>
  </div>
  <button class="hero-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('lessons').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Begin Training ↓</button>
  <div class="scroll-hint"><div class="scroll-line"></div>Scroll to start</div>
</section>

<div class="module-wrap">

  <!-- OBJECTIVE -->
  <div class="section-header" style="padding-top:60px;">
    <div class="s-eyebrow">The Objective</div>
    <h2 class="s-title">One Goal. One Outcome.</h2>
    <p class="s-sub">Every T.I. has exactly one measure of success: <strong style="color:var(--white)">a scheduled appointment that shows.</strong> Not a membership sold. Not a question answered. An appointment. Everything in this module serves that single outcome.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-rule">
    <div class="callout-label">The Prime Directive</div>
    Answer enough to quench their thirst for information — without satisfying their curiosity enough to stay home.
  </div>

  <!-- LESSONS -->
  <div class="section-header" id="lessons">
    <div class="s-eyebrow">Core Lessons</div>
    <h2 class="s-title">8 Elements of a<br>Dominant T.I.</h2>
    <p class="s-sub">Work through every lesson in order. Each one builds on the last. The certification unlocks when you reach the end.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-grid" id="lessonGrid">

    <!-- L1 -->
    <div class="lesson-card" data-lesson="1">
      <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson(this)">
        <span class="lesson-num">01</span>
        <span class="lesson-title-text">The Mindset Before You Dial</span>
        <span class="lesson-tag">Foundation</span>
        <em class="lesson-arrow">▼</em>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson-body">
        <p>Before you say a single word, your internal state is already communicating. Confidence, enthusiasm, and belief in City Fitness are not optional accessories — they are the product. When a prospect calls, they are evaluating whether this place is worth their time, their money, and their commitment. Your energy is the first answer they get.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-tip">
          <div class="callout-label">Pro Tip — The Mirror Rule</div>
          Place a small mirror near the phone. Smile at yourself before you answer. This is not gimmicky — it is physiologically proven. When you smile, your vocal cords relax, your pitch becomes warmer, and customers report a measurably better impression of the person they are speaking with.
        </div>
        <p>Before each call, take 5 seconds to reset. Remind yourself: <em>"City Fitness has everything this person needs."</em> That belief will transfer through the phone faster than any script.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-rule">
          <div class="callout-label">Self-Talk Anchors</div>
          "I am the guide they have been looking for." &nbsp;·&nbsp; "City Fitness changes lives." &nbsp;·&nbsp; "My job is to help, not to sell."
        </div>
        <p>The moment you frame the T.I. as a selling call, you have already lost it. Frame it as a discovery conversation where your only job is to get them excited to come in.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- L2 -->
    <div class="lesson-card" data-lesson="2">
      <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson(this)">
        <span class="lesson-num">02</span>
        <span class="lesson-title-text">The Opening — Energetic, Clear, In Control</span>
        <span class="lesson-tag">First 10 Seconds</span>
        <em class="lesson-arrow">▼</em>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson-body">
        <p>The tone, pace, and clarity of your voice in the first 10 seconds forms the prospect's entire impression of City Fitness. You are the brand. There is no second chance at a first impression on a phone call.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-script">
          <div class="callout-label">Opening Script</div>
          "It's a great day at City Fitness in [Location]. How can I help you today?"
        </div>
        <p>What makes this opening powerful: it is <strong>energetic</strong> ("It's a great day"), it is <strong>specific</strong> (your location), and it is <strong>open</strong> ("How can I help"). You have set a tone of warmth and competence before they have said a word.</p>
        <p>The most common trap here is answering on autopilot — flat tone, rushed delivery, sounding like you have answered this call 400 times. Every call is the first call for that person. Match their moment.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-warn">
          <div class="callout-label">What to Avoid</div>
          Mumbling the club name. Skipping the location. Starting with "Um..." or "Hello?" with rising inflection. Sounding bored before they have said anything.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- L3 -->
    <div class="lesson-card" data-lesson="3">
      <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson(this)">
        <span class="lesson-num">03</span>
        <span class="lesson-title-text">Respond & Ask — Taking Conversational Control</span>
        <span class="lesson-tag">The Pivot</span>
        <em class="lesson-arrow">▼</em>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson-body">
        <p>Nearly every T.I. starts with the prospect asking you a direct question: <em>"How much is a membership?"</em> or <em>"What are your hours?"</em> or <em>"Do you have yoga classes?"</em> This is the moment most consultants lose control of the conversation — and the appointment — before it even starts.</p>
        <p>The framework is simple: <strong>Acknowledge → Pivot → Ask.</strong> Never answer directly and let the conversation stall. Never ignore their question (that feels dismissive). Acknowledge that their question is valid, and immediately redirect with a question of your own.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-script">
          <div class="callout-label">The Control Script</div>
          "Great question — we actually custom tailor memberships based on your specific interests, so there's no one-size-fits-all answer. First, can I get your name? I'm [Your Name], nice to meet you! And tell me a little bit about your situation."
        </div>
        <p>Notice what this does: it validates their question, positions City Fitness as personalized and premium, gets their name (critical for rapport), gives your name (reciprocity), and hands control of the conversation back to you with <em>"tell me about your situation."</em></p>
        <div class="callout callout-rule">
          <div class="callout-label">The Law of the T.I.</div>
          Whoever asks the questions controls the conversation. Your job is to be the one asking.
        </div>
        <p>Every time the prospect asks a question and you answer it with a clean close — no follow-up question from you — they hold the power. Keep pivoting. Keep asking.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- L4 -->
    <div class="lesson-card" data-lesson="4">
      <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson(this)">
        <span class="lesson-num">04</span>
        <span class="lesson-title-text">"Tell Me About Your Situation" — Deep Listening</span>
        <span class="lesson-tag">Discovery</span>
        <em class="lesson-arrow">▼</em>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson-body">
        <p>This is the most underrated line in the entire T.I. Four words that open an entire world of information about who this person is and why they are calling. <em>"Tell me about your situation."</em></p>
        <p>The beauty of this question is its breadth. You are not asking <em>"What are your goals?"</em> — that signals you want to pitch. You are not asking <em>"Have you been to a gym before?"</em> — that's a closed yes/no. You are asking them to tell their story. And people always tell you what matters most to them when they tell their own story.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-rule">
          <div class="callout-label">The Listening Standard</div>
          Do NOT interrupt. Do NOT try to formulate your response while they are still talking. Just listen. The better listener you are, the more trust you build — and on a phone call, trust is everything.
        </div>
        <p>What you are listening for:</p>
        <p><strong>Fitness history:</strong> Are they coming back after time off? Brand new? Been active and looking for a change? This helps you identify their Bucket before they even walk in.</p>
        <p><strong>Emotional clues:</strong> Are they frustrated? Motivated by fear? Excited? The emotion behind their words is more valuable than the words themselves.</p>
        <p><strong>X-Factor hints:</strong> You will not push for the X-Factor on the T.I. — that is saved for the in-person — but you will often hear the seeds of it. A mention of a health scare. A comment about a big event coming up. A reference to a relationship. File it away.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-tip">
          <div class="callout-label">Pro Tip — Find Common Ground</div>
          As they talk, actively look for shared interests or experiences you can briefly acknowledge. "Oh, I actually know exactly what you mean about getting back into a routine after the holidays." A moment of genuine connection turns a cold call into a warm conversation.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- L5 -->
    <div class="lesson-card" data-lesson="5">
      <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson(this)">
        <span class="lesson-num">05</span>
        <span class="lesson-title-text">Goals & The X-Factor — How Deep Do You Go?</span>
        <span class="lesson-tag">Motivation</span>
        <em class="lesson-arrow">▼</em>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson-body">
        <p>Yes, you ask about goals on the T.I. No, you do not go all the way to the X-Factor. Here is the distinction and why it matters.</p>
        <p>Goals give you content — what to highlight in your verbal tour, which membership makes sense, how to frame the appointment. Goals are practical and you need them. Ask for them.</p>
        <p>The X-Factor — the deep, personal <em>why</em> behind the goal — requires trust and physical presence to unlock properly. On a phone call, many people will not go there. And if you push too hard and it feels invasive, you lose the rapport you just built. Leave the deep X-Factor work for the in-person tour where body language, eye contact, and environment all work in your favor.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-script">
          <div class="callout-label">Goals Bridge Script</div>
          "That's great context — it really helps me recommend the right direction for you. What are the main goals you're working toward? Just so I can point you toward the parts of the club that are going to matter most for you."
        </div>
        <p>The phrase <em>"point you toward the parts of the club that matter most for you"</em> is doing important work here. It frames your goal question as service, not interrogation. You are gathering information to help them — and they feel that.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-warn">
          <div class="callout-label">The Trap — Over-Discovery on the Phone</div>
          If you start asking probing personal questions about their weight, health history, or emotional relationship with fitness over the phone before rapport is established, they will pull back. Save the depth for when they are sitting across from you.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- L6 -->
    <div class="lesson-card" data-lesson="6">
      <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson(this)">
        <span class="lesson-num">06</span>
        <span class="lesson-title-text">The Verbal Tour — Building Anticipation with Words</span>
        <span class="lesson-tag">Excitement</span>
        <em class="lesson-arrow">▼</em>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson-body">
        <p>This is where most consultants completely skip a massive opportunity. After you have learned something about what this person needs, you have enough to paint a picture. Not a features list — a picture. Use emotionally charged adjectives. Make them see themselves inside City Fitness before they set foot in it.</p>
        <p>The verbal tour should be short (60–90 seconds), targeted to what they just told you, and designed to spark one specific emotion: <em>I need to see this in person.</em></p>
        <div class="dialogue-block">
          <div class="dialogue-title">Verbal Tour — "Getting Back Into It" Scenario</div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker consultant">Consultant</span><span class="line-text">"Based on what you just told me — getting back into a consistent routine and wanting something that actually feels worth the drive — I think you're going to love what we have here. <em>We have a massive open floor that never feels crowded, top-of-the-line equipment that's always maintained, and honestly the cleanest facility you'll find in the city. Plus our studio class schedule is stacked — yoga, cycling, functional training — a ton of options depending on your mood that day.</em> And honestly the vibe here... it's hard to describe over the phone. It's welcoming but it pushes you. Does it sound like we might have what you're looking for?"</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>Notice the structure: <strong>reflect their need → paint the picture with specific adjectives → connect to their goals → confirm with a tie-down question.</strong></p>
        <div class="callout callout-rule">
          <div class="callout-label">The Tie-Down to End the Verbal Tour</div>
          Always close the verbal tour with a confirming question: "Does it sound like we might have what you're looking for?" or "Does that kind of environment sound like what you've been missing?" You want them to say yes before you ask for the appointment.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- L7 -->
    <div class="lesson-card" data-lesson="7">
      <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson(this)">
        <span class="lesson-num">07</span>
        <span class="lesson-title-text">Scheduling the Appointment — Alternate of Choice</span>
        <span class="lesson-tag">The Close</span>
        <em class="lesson-arrow">▼</em>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson-body">
        <p>This is the close of the T.I. and it is where nervous energy kills more appointments than any other single factor. The moment you go to book the appointment, your tone must stay <strong>exactly the same</strong> as it has been the entire call. If it shifts — if it drops in energy, speeds up, or goes tentative — the prospect feels the pressure and instinctively resists.</p>
        <p>The technique is called <strong>Alternate of Choice</strong>. Instead of asking <em>"When do you want to come in?"</em> — which opens the door to "I'll call you back when I know my schedule" — you offer two specific options that both result in an appointment. You have turned an open-ended decision into a multiple-choice test where every answer is a win.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-script">
          <div class="callout-label">Alternate of Choice Sequence</div>
          "Based on everything you shared, I'd love to set aside some dedicated time for you — no interruptions, just us. Would today work or would tomorrow be better?" [Wait] "Great — would you prefer earlier in the day or evening is usually better?" [Wait] "Perfect — I could do [Time A] or [Time B], which works better?"
        </div>
        <p>Three rounds of choice. Each round narrows down to a specific time. By the third question, you are no longer asking <em>if</em> they are coming — that has been decided. You are only deciding <em>when.</em></p>
        <div class="callout callout-warn">
          <div class="callout-label">The Vocal Shift — The Appointment Killer</div>
          The moment you shift from conversational warmth to "appointment booking mode" — dropping energy, slowing down like you are reading from a form — the prospect detects the transition and their guard goes up. Keep the same voice you have used the entire call. The booking is a natural next step, not a different phase.
        </div>
        <div class="dialogue-block">
          <div class="dialogue-title">Full Appointment Booking Sequence</div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker consultant">Consultant</span><span class="line-text">"This sounds like a great fit — I genuinely think you're going to be impressed when you see it. Let me set aside some dedicated time for you so you get my full attention. <em>Would today work or would tomorrow be easier?</em>"</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker prospect">Prospect</span><span class="line-text">"Tomorrow works."</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker consultant">Consultant</span><span class="line-text">"Perfect. <em>Earlier in the afternoon or evening is usually better for you?</em>"</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker prospect">Prospect</span><span class="line-text">"Evening, probably after 6."</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker consultant">Consultant</span><span class="line-text">"Great. <em>I could do 6:00 or 6:30 — which do you prefer?</em>"</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker prospect">Prospect</span><span class="line-text">"6:30 works."</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker consultant">Consultant</span><span class="line-text">"6:30 tomorrow it is. I'm setting aside that time specifically for you — we won't be interrupted. I'm really looking forward to helping you take that first step. Can I grab your last name and best phone number in case anything changes on my end?"</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- L8 -->
    <div class="lesson-card" data-lesson="8">
      <div class="lesson-header" onclick="toggleLesson(this)">
        <span class="lesson-num">08</span>
        <span class="lesson-title-text">Front Desk T.I. Protocol — Your Role in the Funnel</span>
        <span class="lesson-tag">Front Desk</span>
        <em class="lesson-arrow">▼</em>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson-body">
        <p>The front desk is the first live human voice a prospect hears. Before a T.I. ever reaches a membership consultant, front desk has already set the tone — or broken it. This protocol is non-negotiable because failing to get a name and number before the transfer means if the call drops, that lead is gone forever.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-rule">
          <div class="callout-label">The Two Rules of Front Desk T.I.</div>
          1. Always get the name and phone number BEFORE placing the caller on hold or transferring. &nbsp;&nbsp;2. Always ask if they have been here before — this tells membership how to calibrate the conversation.
        </div>
        <div class="dialogue-block">
          <div class="dialogue-title">Script — First-Time Caller</div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker prospect">Prospect</span><span class="line-text">"Hi, how much are your memberships?"</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker front-desk">Front Desk</span><span class="line-text"><em>"Thank you for calling City Fitness! Let me connect you with our membership team — they'll have all the right information for you. Have you been to City Fitness before?"</em></span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker prospect">Prospect</span><span class="line-text">"No, first time."</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker front-desk">Front Desk</span><span class="line-text"><em>"Great, welcome! Can I grab your name? And your best phone number? Perfect — is it okay if I put you on a brief hold while I connect you? It'll just be a second."</em></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="dialogue-block">
          <div class="dialogue-title">Script — Returning Caller</div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker prospect">Prospect</span><span class="line-text">"Hi, I have a question about upgrading my membership."</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker front-desk">Front Desk</span><span class="line-text"><em>"Of course, let me get you connected with membership! Have you been to our location before?"</em></span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker prospect">Prospect</span><span class="line-text">"Yeah, I was in about a year ago."</span></div>
          <div class="line"><span class="speaker front-desk">Front Desk</span><span class="line-text"><em>"Perfect, do you remember who you spoke with? No worries if not. Can I grab your name and number real quick, and I'll get you right over to the team — one moment."</em></span></div>
        </div>
        <p>The reason you collect name and phone number before the transfer is not bureaucratic — it is protective. If the call drops during transfer, you still have a lead. If the consultant is busy and you have to take a message, you have a lead. Every caller who hangs up without leaving their information is a lost opportunity that never has to happen.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- end lesson-grid -->

