  <div class="section-header" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="s-eyebrow">What Kills the Appointment</div>
    <h2 class="s-title">6 T.I. Mistakes &<br>How to Fix Them</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="mistakes-grid">
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label bad">✗ The Mistake</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">Answering the price question directly: <strong>"Our memberships start at $39.99."</strong> — Now they have what they called for and no reason to come in.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label good">✓ The Fix</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">Acknowledge and pivot: <strong>"We actually custom tailor every membership — there is no one-size-fits-all here. Can I grab your name first?"</strong></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label bad">✗ The Mistake</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">Giving a full feature rundown unprompted: listing every amenity, every class, every price tier. <strong>You have satisfied their curiosity and eliminated any reason to visit.</strong></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label good">✓ The Fix</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">Target the verbal tour to only what they told you they care about. <strong>Leave things out on purpose.</strong> Curiosity is the engine that drives the appointment.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label bad">✗ The Mistake</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">Asking <strong>"When do you want to come in?"</strong> — an open question that invites "I'll have to check my schedule and call you back."</div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label good">✓ The Fix</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">Always use <strong>Alternate of Choice</strong>. Two specific options. Never an open-ended ask. The question is always <em>when</em>, never <em>if</em>.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label bad">✗ The Mistake</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">The <strong>vocal shift</strong> — energy drops, pace slows, tone becomes transactional the moment you move toward booking the appointment.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label good">✓ The Fix</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">The booking is a natural next step in a warm conversation, not a mode switch. <strong>Maintain the exact same energy from start to finish.</strong></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label bad">✗ The Mistake</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">Front desk transfers without getting name and number first. Call drops during transfer. <strong>Lead is gone forever.</strong></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label good">✓ The Fix</div>
      <div class="mistake-text"><strong>Name and number before hold, every single time.</strong> No exceptions. This is the one non-negotiable of the front desk protocol.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label bad">✗ The Mistake</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">Skipping the recap after booking: hanging up without confirming the time, expressing genuine excitement, and setting the expectation of dedicated time.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake-card">
      <div class="mistake-label good">✓ The Fix</div>
      <div class="mistake-text">Always close with: <strong>"I'm setting aside that time specifically for you — we won't be disrupted. I'm looking forward to it."</strong> This plants an obligation and builds anticipation.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

