  <div class="section-header" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="s-eyebrow">Practice</div>
    <h2 class="s-title">4 Scenarios to<br>Train On</h2>
    <p class="s-sub">These are the four most common T.I. call types. Practice each one until your responses are fluid, warm, and automatic — not rehearsed-sounding.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="roleplay-card">
    <div class="roleplay-header">
      <span class="roleplay-scenario">Scenario 01 — The Price Hunter</span>
      <span class="roleplay-type">Most Common</span>
    </div>
    <div class="roleplay-body">
      <div class="roleplay-prospect">"Hi, I just wanted to get some pricing information on your memberships."</div>
      <div class="roleplay-objective"><strong>Your objective:</strong> Do not give them a number. Acknowledge the request, pivot to gathering their name and situation, deliver a targeted verbal tour, and book an appointment using Alternate of Choice.</div>
      <div class="coaching-note">The urge to just answer the price question feels like good customer service. It is not. It is giving away the one reason they have to come in. Resist it. Your response to pricing questions is a system, not improvisation.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="roleplay-card">
    <div class="roleplay-header">
      <span class="roleplay-scenario">Scenario 02 — The Class Inquirer</span>
      <span class="roleplay-type">Studio Interest</span>
    </div>
    <div class="roleplay-body">
      <div class="roleplay-prospect">"Hey, do you guys have yoga? I've been looking for a good yoga class near me."</div>
      <div class="roleplay-objective"><strong>Your objective:</strong> Confirm you have yoga (yes, answer that), but use it as the entry point to discover more about them — why yoga, what they are dealing with (stress, flexibility, recovery?), and what else they might benefit from. Build a targeted verbal tour around their answer and book.</div>
      <div class="coaching-note">This caller has handed you their interest on a plate. Your job is to expand it: "Yes, we have amazing yoga — tell me more about what you're looking for. Is this more for stress relief, flexibility, or just something you've always wanted to try?" Let them talk. Then paint the picture.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="roleplay-card">
    <div class="roleplay-header">
      <span class="roleplay-scenario">Scenario 03 — The Comparison Shopper</span>
      <span class="roleplay-type">Competitive</span>
    </div>
    <div class="roleplay-body">
      <div class="roleplay-prospect">"I'm looking at a few different gyms in the area. What makes you guys different from the others?"</div>
      <div class="roleplay-objective"><strong>Your objective:</strong> Do not trash competitors. Do not list a wall of features. Ask what they have seen so far and what has impressed or disappointed them — then speak only to the gaps. Your differentiators should feel like a direct response to their specific unmet needs, not a marketing brochure.</div>
      <div class="coaching-note">The comparison shopper is actually your best prospect — they are actively in decision mode. Ask: "What have you seen so far, and is there something you're finding that the other places don't quite nail?" Whatever they say, that is your opening. Speak directly to that void.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="roleplay-card">
    <div class="roleplay-header">
      <span class="roleplay-scenario">Scenario 04 — The Reluctant Booker</span>
      <span class="roleplay-type">Objection Handling</span>
    </div>
    <div class="roleplay-body">
      <div class="roleplay-prospect">"I mean, it sounds good, but I'm pretty busy. Can you just send me some info by email?"</div>
      <div class="roleplay-objective"><strong>Your objective:</strong> Acknowledge the request warmly, do not argue against it, but use the Offer Alternative principle — offer to send a follow-up AND book a quick appointment. "I will absolutely send you some info. And given what you shared, I think five minutes in person will show you more than a brochure ever could. I could fit you in quickly tomorrow — would 5:30 or 6:00 be easier?"</div>
      <div class="coaching-note">The email request is almost always a soft "not ready yet" — not a hard no. The fix is not to fight it. Agree, then layer the appointment on top. A short, low-pressure ask alongside the email agreement removes the resistance entirely.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

